Competitive Landscape
The global immersive equipment market is characterized by "technology-intensiv competition". While the market features global leaders, it remains fragmented with opportunities for specialized manufacturers. The top six companies collectively accounted for 27.31% of the total global revenue in 2024. This structure indicates a decentralized industry phase where small and medium-sized technology firms can carve out niches, particularly in areas such as special effects integration and custom content development.
Leading firms fortify their positions through technical patent barriers and strategic IP content cooperation. For instance, Fantawild leverages its proprietary "Boonie Bears" IP to create distinctive flying theater experiences, while Brogent secures its high-end market position through its combined dynamic track and VR solutions. As industry standards evolve and technology investment thresholds rise, market leaders are anticipated to further consolidate supply chain resources. However, regional manufacturers maintain competitive edges through localized service capabilities and cost-efficient production models, ensuring a diverse and dynamic competitive environment.
